WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.791 --> 00:00:04.041
(dramatic organ music)

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.473
- Good morning.

00:00:54.384 --> 00:00:56.452
- [Audience] Good morning.

00:00:56.452 --> 00:00:59.040
- Class of '22, transfer students,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.090
and Eli Whitney students, welcome to Yale!

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.257
(audience applauding)

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.000
Joining me on this stage to greet you are

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.140
President Peter Salovey,
Provost Ben Polak,

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.460
Secretary Kim Goff-Crews,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.530
Faculty of Arts and
Sciences Dean Tamar Gendler,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.840
Graduate School Dean Lynn Cooley,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.000
Athletic Director Vicky Chun,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.910
Association of Yale Alumni
Executive Director Weili Cheng,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:35.710
officers of the university,

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.420
the heads and deans of
the residential colleges,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.200
and the deans and members of
the Yale College Dean's Office.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.520
We extend our warm welcome to
the family members and friends

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.670
who are here today.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.490
It is thanks to your support and guidance

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.993
that Yale has the privilege

00:01:53.993 --> 00:01:57.523
of hosting these impressive young adults.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.060
I am Marvin Chun, dean of Yale College,

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.163
and I am extremely
pleased to be here today.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.880
In my role as dean, one of my jobs is

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.950
to support President Salovey's mission

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.530
to maintain Yale as the research
institution most committed

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.310
to teaching and learning,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.250
and what that means for you
students is that I am here

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.340
to make sure that you get
the best out of this place,

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.433
in the classroom or out of it.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.010
Before I address you as dean, though,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.240
let me first step out of that role

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.730
and into another role that I play here,

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.670
as a professor of
psychology and neuroscience.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.120
I taught here for 18 years,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.060
and in fact this spring I will be teaching

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.923
Introduction to Psychology.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.680
I hope you may take it.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.020
But I have to confess, that
according to course evaluations,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.320
which I encourage you to consider

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.970
when you shop for the many
courses you'll be taking here,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.955
my class is apparently overrated.

00:02:58.955 --> 00:03:01.970
(audience laughing)

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.010
But being the resilient
optimist that I am,

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.060
I actually consider that as a compliment.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.870
But as a professor, then,
let me get down to business

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.690
and give you your first quiz,

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.330
a friendly one with no grading,
I heard the groan. (laughs)

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.138
So no grading, to get started,

00:03:20.138 --> 00:03:22.600
please pull out the
insert in your program.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:23.733
Looks like this.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.630
And we're gonna focus on the top row.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.300
The top of the program.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:36.170
You see four cards
depicted on the top row.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.850
And imagine that each card has a letter

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:43.840
or digit on, a letter
or digit on each side.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.483
And you're only seeing
one side on the top row.

00:03:47.882 --> 00:03:50.510
And I want you to look at these cards

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.220
and consider the following rule.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.250
If the card has a vowel on one side,

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.623
then it has an even number on the other.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.840
Okay, so if a card has
a vowel on one side,

00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:07.610
then it has an even number on the other.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.360
And we wrote down the rule
on the bottom of the card.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.090
So here's the quiz.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:17.090
Which card or which cards
do you need to flip over

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.200
in order to determine if
the rule is true or false?

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.330
And again, the rule is, if the
card has a vowel on one side,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.580
then it has an even
number on the other side.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.000
Okay, so give that some thought.

00:04:48.808 --> 00:04:49.641
All right.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.970
Yale is a very collaborative place,

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.520
but on most quizzes you're not allowed

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:57.952
to talk to your neighbor.

00:04:57.952 --> 00:04:58.785
(audience laughing)

00:04:58.785 --> 00:05:01.230
But it's fine for this morning, obviously.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.410
So here's the correct answer.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:07.050
And thank you for
engaging in the exercise.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:08.760
So here's the correct answer.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.760
The correct answer is to pick
the A card and the seven card.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.460
In the original study only

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.433
about one out of 25
people got this correct.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.130
Now while I expect that many more

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.270
of you got the right answer,

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.900
I will not ask you to raise your hand

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.074
because I don't want you to be bragging

00:05:31.074 --> 00:05:32.723
on the first day of
school, it's not very cool.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.070
But please do not feel bad
if you didn't get it right

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.390
because if this demo were
easy, or intuitive or obvious,

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.410
it would not be instructive.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:44.770
So let's go through it together.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.670
When people take this quiz,

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.040
most people correctly pick the A card

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.100
because if you turn it over

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.610
and see an odd number,
you've disproven the rule.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.390
Most people get the A card.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.240
The D card, the second
card, is irrelevant,

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.570
and almost no one picks that one.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.340
What makes this quiz interesting is

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.030
that a lot of people
do pick the four card,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.320
even though they don't need to,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:11.250
because that card is
irrelevant to the rule.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.053
People still pick it because
it lets them confirm the rule,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:18.050
if they find a vowel on the other side,

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.270
then they're like, oh yeah,
the rule is confirmed.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.300
But this card, the four card,

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.590
does not allow you to
prove the rule false.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.390
The four card can be flipped over

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:32.320
and show a consonant and
the rule would still hold,

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.573
because we said nothing about consonants.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.520
The only card that lets you
test if the rule is false

00:06:39.646 --> 00:06:41.620
is the seven card because
if you flip that one over

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.323
and see a vowel, then that
would contradict the rule.

00:06:45.323 --> 00:06:47.792
(person speaking faintly)
Thank you.

00:06:47.792 --> 00:06:49.020
(audience laughing)

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:49.853
That's good.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.890
So the finding that people
choose the four card

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:58.160
and not the seven card
has a technical term.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.570
And it is known as confirmation bias.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:03.450
And what it means is that people lean

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.990
towards validating their hypotheses

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.050
rather than testing if they're wrong.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.940
Put another way, people
are drawn to evidence

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.003
that confirms their pre-existing beliefs.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:19.230
And so this is something
we want to counter

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.240
and you would think that in order

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.521
to counter confirmation bias,

00:07:24.521 --> 00:07:26.670
all you would need to
do is for information

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.343
that goes against your preexisting views.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.090
Unfortunately, it's not that easy.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.310
'Cause people don't like to do that.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.520
In another study, students were surveyed

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.580
and divided into two different groups.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.480
One that favored capital
punishment another group

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.850
that was surveyed to disfavor,

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.580
they were against capitol punishment.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:48.630
Once they got these two groups,

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.900
identified them according to their views

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.920
and then you presented both groups

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.050
with two types of evidence.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.800
One type of evidence that supports,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.490
that favors capital punishment
as a deterrent to crime,

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.290
and another set of evidence that shows

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:09.020
that capitol punishment is not
effective at deterring crime.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.113
And you show these both types
of evidence to both groups.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.630
And you think, okay, that's how,

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.440
education's easy, that's how it works.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.390
And you would think that seeing
both sides would moderate

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.400
each subject's view,

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.340
but what the researchers
found was disheartening.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.100
It was actually the opposite,

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.280
that instead the students in each group,

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.460
they started to dig in,

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.170
they favored the evidence
that supported their view,

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.470
they started to devalue

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:38.100
and discount the evidence that didn't.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.110
And it left both sides even more polarized

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:42.460
than when they had started.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.993
That is, being exposed to
multiple views is not sufficient.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:54.683
Instead, one needs to see how
opposing views are also valid.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.750
So let me try to
demonstrate this point again

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.580
using the insert in your program.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.970
Here's your second
exercise of the morning.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.120
You're gonna look at the image

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:06.600
in the middle of the insert now.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.840
Okay, take a look at
the image in the middle.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.330
When I say go, name out loud what you see.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:15.400
And actually you can feel free to use

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.380
whatever language you want,

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.250
if English is not your first language.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:19.083
Here we go.

00:09:19.083 --> 00:09:20.450
So what do you see, go.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.400
- Duck!
- Rabbit!

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:27.860
- Okay, so, if you first
saw a duck, raise your hand.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.090
Raise your hand if you saw a duck.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:30.380
Okay and look around.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.400
Now raise your hand if you saw a rabbit.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.190
Okay, right, almost half of
you have different answers

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.784
to this demonstration.

00:09:38.784 --> 00:09:42.010
(people chattering)

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.070
And you can feel free to see

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:44.550
if you can you see it both ways?

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.432
Help your partner see it the other way.

00:09:47.432 --> 00:09:50.515
(people chattering)

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:03.363
Okay, and thank you.

00:10:03.363 --> 00:10:05.990
So everyone sees it both ways now?

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:06.823
Okay, good.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.210
This simple demonstration shows

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:13.210
a fundamental principle of psychology,

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.710
which is that we all experience
the world differently.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:19.440
Our perceptual
interpretations, our memories,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.750
our thoughts, are all
constructs of the mind,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.580
and they differ across people

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.780
even when you're viewing the same object,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.443
the same event, or the same evidence.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.050
When exposed to views
different from your own,

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.890
it helps if you can find common ground

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.193
with those with whom you disagree.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:42.860
Before you put away your insert,

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.540
let's take one last look at it,

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.953
this time at the cartoon on the bottom.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:51.600
You see two armies face each other,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.480
and now look at the army on the bottom,

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:56.050
and at the front of it there's
a general at the front of it,

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.960
sitting on a horse, urging his troops,

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:02.450
and this is the cartoon caption.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:06.550
There can be no peace until
they renounce their Rabbit God

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.453
and accept our Duck God.

00:11:08.453 --> 00:11:10.534
(audience laughing)

00:11:10.534 --> 00:11:12.384
You can see the flags and everything.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.260
So of course, imagine what happens

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.840
when the opposing troops realize

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.173
that they are serving the same flag.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:24.690
And so I would like to ask
you, what is your flag?

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.150
What is the flag that you serve.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.193
And so please shout out the
answer to the following.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.809
What's the best university in the world?

00:11:33.809 --> 00:11:35.527
- [Crowd] Yale!

00:11:35.527 --> 00:11:38.537
(audience laughing)

00:11:38.537 --> 00:11:39.600
- Okay, let me try that again.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.799
What's the best university in the world?

00:11:41.799 --> 00:11:42.632
- [Crowd] Yale!

00:11:42.632 --> 00:11:43.850
- Thank, all right great.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.113
You are all now members of Bulldog Nation.

00:11:47.113 --> 00:11:50.363
(audience applauding)

00:11:56.764 --> 00:11:58.240
Thank you.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.060
With the sophomores, and
juniors, and seniors,

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:04.294
the alumni, faculty, and staff
and with each other here,

00:12:04.294 --> 00:12:07.040
you have common ground,

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.610
an immediate shared bond
that is more than tribal.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.710
It goes deeper, to the
intellectual curiosity

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.770
and the commitment to community

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.583
that have brought you all here.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.520
You have just arrived at a
place that values difference

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.970
and actively seeks it out.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:28.670
It is how professors and
students find and test knowledge

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.880
and it how the university
equips its students, that's you,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.130
to become leaders and
citizens of the world.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.230
But here, the value of
seeking difference is

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.560
even more than that,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.070
here it is taken as an article of faith,

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:42.680
it is the coin of the realm,

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.380
it is one of the bedrock principles

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.393
that governs us, in and
out of the classroom.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:51.260
And let me assume that
you came here not just

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:54.010
to discover whole realms of knowledge,

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:57.283
but also to learn how to
live lives of consequence.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:12:59.773
If that is true,

00:12:59.773 --> 00:13:01.500
then you have really
come to the right place.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:03.140
As you take your first steps, then,

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.430
you will need to become
comfortable with difference,

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:10.160
not just to accept it or
tolerate it but to insist on it.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:11.910
And you will find guides everywhere,

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:15.290
in the classes you will take,
the activities you will join,

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.060
and most of all from the people sitting

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:20.240
around you right now,
your peers and classmates.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:21.220
All of them are part

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.520
of the liberal arts
education that awaits you.

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.040
And what you will find is that you come

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.530
from every possible walk of life,

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:33.120
bringing with you every imaginable talent,

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.413
every experience, every worldview.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.623
Almost half of you identify
as a student of color,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:43.850
12% African American, 22% Asian American,

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:47.563
15% Hispanic, Latinx, 2% Native American.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.530
About one out of nine of you
are international students

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:54.980
from 57 different countries in this class.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.390
You are of all genders and sexualities.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:01.683
About two out of three of you
attended public high schools.

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:04.990
To get here, you have overcome challenges

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.683
and also disabilities.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.720
You observe different religions or none.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:13.833
You are politically liberal,
conservative, moderate.

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:16.210
Over one out of six

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:19.920
of you are first generation
college students.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.910
Nearly one out of five of you qualify

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:24.820
for Pell grants for low income families.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:27.211
Yale is really a great
place, so thank you.

00:14:27.211 --> 00:14:30.378
(audience applauding)

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.660
Speaking of different experiences,

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.882
have any of you seen the
movie Crazy, Rich Asians?

00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:45.299
(audience applauding)

00:14:45.299 --> 00:14:50.299
Not me of course, but I wanted to mention

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:52.540
that if you wanted a
different kind experience,

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.910
kind of see the lifestyle
that's portrayed in the movie,

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.584
consider a semester abroad
at Yale-NUS College.

00:14:58.584 --> 00:15:01.770
(audience applauding)

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:03.500
Our sister in Singapore

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.050
which is just a really fabulous, fun city

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:08.593
and you can get really
terrific chilli crab there.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:13.050
But also just an ad to
consider spending time abroad,

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.600
at any one of our hundreds
of partners around the world.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.750
Maybe in a spring semester
of your sophomore year

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.315
when you should not feel
any fear of missing out

00:15:21.315 --> 00:15:23.510
for the winter temperatures

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.993
and two feet of snow on the ground here.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.410
Your life at Yale has just started,

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:32.220
and in just a few days you
will be enrolling in classes,

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:35.470
joining organizations, and
training with your teams.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.350
And as you do that, here's
what I hope you will do,

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:40.460
beware of confirmation bias,

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:42.450
when you find yourself simply validating

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.040
what you already know.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:47.640
If you are a duck person,
go find those rabbit people,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.950
and find common ground with them.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.750
If you are studying all duck courses,

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:56.040
go find the rabbit courses, and
see what they can teach you.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.430
And when you find yourself
rallying around a duck flag,

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.860
take a good, hard look at the
rabbit flag across the field

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:05.020
and the people rallying around it,

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:07.523
and remember that you are all Yalies.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.690
So Eli Whitney students,
transfer students,

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.150
members of the Class of 2022,

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:18.199
you all belong to Yale, Yale
belongs to you, welcome.

00:16:18.199 --> 00:16:21.366
(audience applauding)

