00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:29.663
- So good morning everyone.

00:20:30.664 --> 00:20:32.620
- [Crowd] Good morning.

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:36.530
- On behalf of my
colleagues here on stage,

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:40.610
I extend a warm welcome
to all the family members

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:44.990
who are with us today
and to the first year's,

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:48.420
transfer students and
Eli Whitney's students.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:49.493
Welcome to Yale.

00:20:50.389 --> 00:20:53.639
(audience applauding)

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:02.363
Today is a day of
pageantry and excitement.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:06.240
Many of you bring members
of your extended families

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:10.900
to cheer you on,
celebrating this milestone

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:15.723
with justifiable pride
and just a little anxiety.

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:21.163
Today is also a day of Yale traditions.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:26.600
You will encounter countless
wonderful rituals here,

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:29.643
some recent and some quite old.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:36.000
Many are steeped in
history yet remain popular,

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:39.800
even beloved, among Yalies.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.723
And remember you are now a Yalie.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:47.320
One of our Yale traditions is singing

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:51.193
an old song, Bright College Years.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:55.470
Written in the late 19th century.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:59.973
it is our unofficial but
widely acknowledged alma mater.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.930
You will hear it at many campus events

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:08.920
often sung by the Yale Glee
Club and other Yale groups

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:12.970
and played by the Yale
Precision Marching Band

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.433
after football games.

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:19.483
Now I am not a singer.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.320
Actually I'm a bluegrass bass player

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:29.603
but I hope you'll indulge
me for just a moment.

00:22:31.904 --> 00:22:36.045
♪ The seasons come, the seasons go ♪

00:22:36.045 --> 00:22:40.754
♪ The earth is green or white with snow ♪

00:22:40.754 --> 00:22:45.754
♪ But time and change shall not avail ♪

00:22:49.427 --> 00:22:54.260
♪ To break the friendships
formed at Yale ♪

00:22:58.305 --> 00:23:01.555
(audience applauding)

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:11.078
So thank you for that.

00:23:11.078 --> 00:23:14.640
Those couplets are some of my favorites

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:18.340
from bright College
Years, lifelong friendship

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:22.050
and in my experience they are truthful.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:25.260
I suspect they will provide accurate,

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:29.600
they will prove accurate for you as well.

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:33.880
But it's the songs final lines,

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:36.710
popular at alumni gatherings

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.110
and always sung with gusto,

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:41.630
complete with the waving of handkerchiefs

00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:46.603
that I want to use to
launch my topic today.

00:23:48.879 --> 00:23:53.223
♪ Oh let us strive that ever we ♪

00:23:53.223 --> 00:23:58.223
♪ May let these words, our watch cry be ♪

00:23:58.319 --> 00:24:03.319
♪ Well upon life's sea, ♪

00:24:04.385 --> 00:24:09.385
♪ we sail for God, for
country and for Yale. ♪

00:24:15.356 --> 00:24:18.606
(audience applauding)

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:30.803
For God, for country and for Yale.

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:36.930
A member of the Yale
College class of 1881 named,

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:40.750
Henry Durant, wrote this ballad

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:45.750
and the final lines were
meant to be a rallying cry.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:50.530
It made sense in those days
to presume, as Duran did,

00:24:50.530 --> 00:24:52.420
that most Yale students shared

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:56.090
or at least professed
to share the same God

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:57.373
and the same country.

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:03.150
Most Yalies until recent
decades were white, Protestant

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:07.970
and American and of
course until 50 years ago

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.993
in Yale College they were all men.

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:16.000
Today Yale is a different place

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.800
from the college Durand knew.

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:21.760
We welcome people from around the world,

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:25.553
from every background and
from every walk of life.

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:29.703
I am proud to be a Yale graduate.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:35.483
I received my PhD in
psychology from Yale in 1986,

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:39.940
a hundred years earlier I
may have been less likely

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:43.880
to have been admitted to Yale
on account of my background.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:47.263
I am Jewish with roots in Eastern Europe.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:51.650
My wife Marta is another
proud Yale graduate

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:53.530
and she received her Master's degree

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:56.110
in Public Health in 1984.

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:58.100
Marta why don't you give a wave here

00:25:58.100 --> 00:25:59.606
so people can see who you are.

00:25:59.606 --> 00:26:02.856
(audience applauding)

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:11.700
So Marta received her Master's
in Public Health in 1984.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:14.553
Her family is from Puerto Rico.

00:26:15.650 --> 00:26:18.803
Now our stories are not unique.

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:22.610
Over the past decades Yale has opened

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:25.473
its doors wider and wider.

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:30.383
We have expanded the circle of belonging.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:35.400
Yet despite our differences and diversity,

00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:39.450
we have at least one very
important thing in common.

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:42.253
We all share Yale.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:46.530
No matter where you are from

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:52.410
or who you are or your
path to arriving here,

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:57.410
now you are among other things
a member of this community.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.170
You belong here.

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:05.040
You are a citizen of Yale.

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:06.750
In our country and in our world today,

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.870
questions about citizenship

00:27:08.870 --> 00:27:12.570
and immigration are hotly contested.

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:16.230
But at Yale we share
none of this uncertainty

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.240
about the critical importance of immigrant

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:22.273
and international students and scholars.

00:27:23.742 --> 00:27:26.992
(audience applauding)

00:27:34.261 --> 00:27:37.920
The work of the university,
education and research,

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:42.600
requires the free movement
of people and ideas

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:45.190
across national boundaries.

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:49.190
So on behalf of this university
I advocate for policies

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:53.050
that will allow us to
welcome students and scholars

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:56.123
from around the world to our campus.

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:01.470
Our Yale citizenship however is not based

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.260
on national origin.

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:08.260
Our students across the university
hail from 121 countries.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:14.870
Nor is it based on our adherence
to a certain set of beliefs

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:19.176
or dogma as we bring an
enormous range of viewpoints

00:28:19.176 --> 00:28:23.530
and perspectives to this campus.

00:28:23.530 --> 00:28:27.410
Instead we are citizens of Yale

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:30.990
because we share a desire to know,

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:34.773
to understand and to create.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:39.400
We are members of an
academic community dedicated

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:41.607
to (speaking in foreign
language) light and truth.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:51.370
We are poets and psychologists,

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:56.370
historians and scientists,
physicians and Dean's,

00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:02.540
and yet we all share the
same fundamental goal,

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:06.490
to expand the horizons of the known world,

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:11.250
to ask questions that shake
the foundations of knowledge

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:14.773
and to rebuild them
again with new answers.

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:20.923
Our world is desperate for
new ideas and solutions.

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:27.010
We need to understand the
human condition and our planet.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.460
We need insights into the genome.

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:32.480
We need breakthroughs in our ability

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:37.430
to fight disease, alleviate
suffering and find justice.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:43.023
We need answers to urgent
long-standing questions.

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:48.223
You will tackle this
important work at Yale.

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:52.350
The experiences you have
here will shape the rest

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:55.940
of your lives and you
will have opportunities

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:58.893
that most people only dream of.

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:05.450
And because a Yale education
is a great privilege,

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:08.163
it comes with certain obligations.

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:10.200
I want to speak today

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:12.980
about some of the most
important obligations

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:14.900
of Yale citizenship.

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:16.733
I will delineate four of them.

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:22.343
The responsibility to
be curious constantly,

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.550
the duty to listen to others,

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:29.200
even those whose thoughts you despise

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:31.293
and to exchange ideas freely.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:36.490
The obligation to create
a culture of respect here

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:43.290
and the requirement to use the
gifts you have been provided

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.633
to serve others and the world.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:51.760
So the first obligation
concerns our intellectual

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:53.820
and scholarly work.

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:57.660
Our campus must be a place
conducive to deep study

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:01.170
that will motivate both
a lifetime of learning

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:02.830
and the development of character

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:06.683
that will serve you
well as future leaders.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.223
Yale will demand much of you.

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:14.710
There will be times when you
don't understand an assignment

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:17.283
or struggle with a problem set.

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.773
You may do poorly on a midterm.

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:23.763
At least I hope so.

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:29.790
Those failures as much as your successes

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:33.383
mean you are doing something right.

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:39.650
Be kind to yourself and remember
that you have come to Yale

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:42.713
because you don't know everything.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:45.661
Not yet.

00:31:45.661 --> 00:31:48.150
(audience laughing)

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:52.790
The faculty will be alongside
you as teachers, as mentors.

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:55.690
You know this is my 33rd
year on the Yale faculty

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:58.830
and I know that working
with students is one

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:01.093
of the great joys of this profession.

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:05.800
So go to office hours, get
to know your professors.

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:10.600
They will help you deepen
and expand your expertise.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:15.600
But most of all allow your
curiosity to take wing,

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:19.170
take you in unexpected directions,

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:23.773
lead you to new areas of
study, practice and discovery.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:27.880
I enrolled in courses in college

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:29.940
that I hadn't planned to take

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:34.040
and they changed the
way I see the world now,

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:35.373
40 years later.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:40.363
A course in the history of theater styles,

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:43.743
a course in writing poetry,

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:49.303
a course in real world applied sociology,

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:54.320
a course in geology and Earth Sciences

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:57.053
that involved fascinating fieldwork.

00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:02.410
Make sure you explore the
great range and diversity

00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:06.523
of academic experiences
available to you here.

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:10.180
Second, as citizens of Yale,

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:14.000
we are obligated to listen
carefully to others.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:15.940
Sometimes this means we must listen

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:18.273
to ideas we find objectionable.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:21.300
You don't have to agree

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:24.560
but each of us must enjoy the opportunity

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.313
to express thoughts and opinions.

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:33.160
We work hard to safeguard
this right on campus.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:37.690
I hope you'll have many
opportunities to think deeply

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:42.690
and speak honestly and
courageously about difficult issues

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.253
during your time here.

00:33:46.510 --> 00:33:50.380
Discourse is the heart of
the academic enterprise,

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:54.090
so find time, find
places for conversation,

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:56.650
whether in a classroom, a dining hall

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:58.303
or on the athletic field.

00:34:00.042 --> 00:34:02.290
There are many impediments

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:06.860
to meaningful conversation,
including technology.

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:08.390
Now I'm not going to persuade you

00:34:08.390 --> 00:34:11.930
to stop texting or tweeting all together

00:34:11.930 --> 00:34:16.480
but I urge you to put down
your phones whenever possible.

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.523
Seek out face-to-face interactions.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:25.630
We're happier and our relationships
are stronger when we do.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:29.870
Just ask anyone who took
Psychology and the Good Life

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:32.643
with Professor Laurie Santos last year.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:37.003
They can tell you the data
that support that observation.

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:42.400
You will meet people of remarkable talent,

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:47.367
remarkable promise and remarkable
integrity here at Yale.

00:34:48.950 --> 00:34:51.430
In the days and weeks
ahead I would urge you

00:34:51.430 --> 00:34:55.610
to seek out a wide variety
of friends and associates.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:58.513
As I said to last year's graduating class,

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:03.170
draw a larger circle to include people

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:08.170
who might look, talk, act or
think differently from you.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:12.580
Introduce yourself to staff members,

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:15.583
get to know your neighbors
in the city of New Haven.

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:20.580
Your ability to speak but also to listen,

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:24.150
to reach beyond what is familiar and easy,

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:26.470
will be one of the great measures

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:28.913
of your time here at Yale.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:33.660
Third, as citizens of Yale we strive

00:35:33.660 --> 00:35:37.750
to support a culture of
mutual respect on our campus.

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:42.420
To do this we must accord
each person the dignity

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:44.413
and recognition they deserve.

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:48.950
Claudia Rankine is the Frederick Iseman,

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:51.023
Professor of Poetry at Yale.

00:35:52.350 --> 00:35:56.880
In her powerful work,
Citizen: An American Lyric,

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:01.000
she explores what citizenship
and belonging mean

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.880
in contemporary America.

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:08.603
Often she does this by
describing mundane situations.

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:11.593
I'd like to read you a passage.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:15.980
In line at the drugstore,

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:20.470
it's finally your turn and then it's not

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.410
as he walks in front of you

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:24.523
and puts his things on the counter.

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:29.887
The cashier says, "Sir, she was next."

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:34.700
When he turns to you
he is truly surprised.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:37.373
Oh my god, I didn't see you.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.410
You must be in a hurry, you offer.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:44.890
No, no, no.

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:47.283
I really didn't see you.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:54.860
So who do we see or not see
in our residential colleges

00:36:55.440 --> 00:37:00.440
and classrooms, in restaurants,
on vacation and at work

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:05.100
in our country and in our world?

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:08.783
Who do we see and who do we look past?

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:13.630
Your lives at Yale will be busy and full.

00:37:13.630 --> 00:37:17.820
You will study, work, volunteer, socialize

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:21.000
and I dearly hope sometimes sleep

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.810
but make sure you take the time

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:27.810
to see the people around you.

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:31.580
Try to imagine the world
through their eyes,

00:37:31.580 --> 00:37:35.913
bring empathy and imagination
to all that you do.

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:40.690
I am counting on each of you.

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:45.350
Together we can ensure
that the Yale community,

00:37:45.350 --> 00:37:48.620
that we can ensure that
Yale is a community

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:52.893
where each person feels
valued and welcomed.

00:37:55.420 --> 00:37:59.740
Finally your obligations
as citizens of Yale extend

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:01.830
beyond the campus.

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:06.280
Our alumni are perhaps
the greatest illustration

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:09.210
of Yale's tradition of service.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:13.930
Five Yale graduates have
served as US presidents,

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:18.930
four as Secretaries of
State and 18 as justices

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:22.180
on the US Supreme Court,

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:25.963
representing viewpoints
across the political spectrum.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:30.410
Yale alumni have served as heads of state

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:32.380
of several other countries

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:37.380
including Italy, Mexico,
Malawi and South Korea.

00:38:39.700 --> 00:38:43.590
Many others have improved
their neighborhoods and cities

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:47.683
as teachers, philanthropists and mentors.

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:52.223
Still others have built
businesses and created jobs.

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:57.600
For generations our students
and alumni have contributed

00:38:57.600 --> 00:38:59.560
to the common good.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:04.407
I urge you to carry on
this vital Yale tradition.

00:39:07.570 --> 00:39:11.243
For God, for country and for Yale.

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:16.820
This is still the promise
of Bright College Years

00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:21.040
and I enjoy singing in those
words as much as anyone

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:26.690
that even if we worship
differently or not at all

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:30.350
even if we are citizens
of different nations

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:32.383
or people without a country,

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:36.433
we all share Yale.

00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:41.770
We take pride in our
rights and responsibilities

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:44.023
as members of this community.

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:49.870
In return for the great
privilege of a Yale education,

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:54.760
we look beyond this campus
to pursue a larger purpose,

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:59.760
to improve the world today
and for future generations.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:03.293
This is what we share in common.

00:40:05.410 --> 00:40:09.110
I'm convinced that our
Yale citizenship is just

00:40:09.110 --> 00:40:14.110
as vital today as it was 317 years ago

00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:16.163
when this college was founded.

00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:22.320
The world needs light and
truth as much as ever.

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:27.583
It needs your leadership and your service.

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:32.910
It needs the meaning
you bring to the world

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.633
and the questions you ask.

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:41.820
Most of all it needs your best efforts,

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:45.513
your successes and your failures.

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:49.750
As citizens of Yale,
sharing a common purpose,

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:53.220
the pursuit of knowledge
and understanding,

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:55.610
let us start today

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:59.990
to begin the work we have come here to do.

00:40:59.990 --> 00:41:04.990
To ask new questions, to listen
carefully and speak honestly

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:11.760
to see with new eyes and to contribute

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:15.363
to our communities and our world.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:21.040
Most of all leave this hall today

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:24.140
with a commitment in your hearts

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:27.053
to be exemplary citizens of Yale.

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:31.933
Building the future we all hope to see.

00:41:32.820 --> 00:41:37.820
Today as I look out onto this room,

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:41.660
I am optimistic about the future of Yale

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:45.523
and the future of our world.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:51.333
Good luck class of 2022.

00:41:51.333 --> 00:41:54.500
(audience applauding)

